# Engineer report: Python C4 slice I3 (publication and DHT import)

Status: COMPLETE. JVM, Node and Dart `yang.python --slow` lanes green.

## Scope ruling

Read: yang.antlr.md 8.5.6 (I3 row and acceptance, cycles ruling 9,
foreign principals ruling 19, L-a..L-f table: L-a declaration half and
L-f landed with P2, L-b/L-c/L-d/L-e open); yin.vm.linker.dht.md 6.1-6.3,
6.6, 7.1-7.4 (R8 near line 345: one address by several declared
principals resolves, different addresses `:ambiguous-name`);
`yin.vm.linker.dht`, `yin.repl.link` (the DHT link source already resolves
by the fold, loads the closure, runs `dependency-bindings` and refuses
`:yin.link.dht/dependency-binding` before any install child); the
`dht-end-to-end-test` mesh pattern.

Deliverable now, without L-e (implemented completely):

1. Publication of Python units by the importer's own principal: a new
   `yang.python.antlr.publish` namespace (AST-level, emits specs through
   `lower/module-spec`, publishes through `yin.vm.linker.publish`) that
   orders a unit set by its static imports, publishes bottom-up pinning
   each dependency's manifest address, and signs one name assertion per
   module (`publish/assertion`, sequences derived as 6.5/6.6 say).
2. A cyclic unit set is refused at publication, before anything is
   written, naming the cycle's Python modules
   (`:yang.python.antlr/import-cycle`). Ruling 9's "first" step; SCC units
   stay I5.
3. DHT import: a publisher node publishes `py` and `pym.m` by name over
   the `dao.jing.dht` mesh seam; a reader node handed the publisher's index
   manifest and declaring its principal runs the parserless-lowered
   importer (the committed CST packet) on the semantic, stack and register
   VMs through the DHT link source; output equals the in-store linked run
   and CPython's.
4. A republished dependency (`k` republished after `t` pinned it) is
   refused as `:yin.link.dht/dependency-binding` `:mismatch` naming
   `pym.k`, with no install child and no Python output. This is existing
   linker behavior (7.4); I3 asserts it under Python.

Needs L-e (refusal path only): an import whose module (or a transitive
dependency) resolves through an asserter that is not the importer's own
declared principal. Ruling 19: until D7 slice B verifies closure origin and
store ownership recursively, a forged `:store-of` could reach `py`'s
state. I3 refuses it explicitly and qualified,
`:yang.python.antlr/foreign-principal`, naming the module and its
asserters, before any link of it. What L-e must add is recorded at the end
of this report.

Deviation (cross-host reading): "a JVM-published `pym.m` imports
parserless on Node and Dart" is read as the design's own acceptance note
says (Node and Dart consume precompiled CST packets): the test is cljc over
the mesh seam (the in-process form `dht-end-to-end-test` already uses for
the linker's L5 gate), and on every host the publisher's input is the
JVM parser's committed packet (checked against the live parser by
`e2e_test`), so the published bytes are the JVM's. No real cross-process
Node/Dart reader exists for Dart at all, and no new hand-pinned address
is added (orchestrator note); host-independence of the manifest bytes
rests on the existing `py` manifest golden asserted on every host.

Also folded in: the I2 `from m import *` gap (indexed `__all__` access).

Ruling choices (recorded as readings, matching the linker doc):

- "The importer's declared principal": the Python link source names its
  own principals (`principal-admission` over public keys); a name is
  admitted when one of them asserts it (it vouches for that exact
  manifest address, R8's provenance may list others too) or when it is a
  direct `:name-env` entry (the composition's own). Anything else is
  refused `:yang.python.antlr/foreign-principal`. An undeclared
  principal's assertion stays the fold's `:absent` with
  `:undeclared-principal` (existing linker behavior); the new refusal is
  for a principal the reader declares but the Python runtime may not
  trust with `py`'s state yet.
- The refusal sits at by-name resolution in the DHT link source, through
  a new optional `:admit` seam on `yin.repl.link/composition` (nil
  admits; a refusal body refuses before any load or link). Every by-name
  request passes it, so a transitive dependency an install child
  requires is refused too.

## Milestones

1. DONE. `__all__` by index. CPython 3.9.6 texts (verified with
   `python3` 3.9.6 on this machine): `'generator' object does not support
   indexing`, `'set' object does not support indexing`, `dict is not a
   sequence`. Prelude: new `py/star-item` (str/list/tuple item by index,
   `:py/stop` past the end; dict "is not a sequence"; anything else
   "'<type>' object does not support indexing"); `py/star-copy-all` reads
   item i, checks and copies it, then reads i+1; `py/import-star` passes
   `__all__` itself, no `py/to-vector`. New program
   `star-all-main-packet` (parser packet, CPython output fixed): RED on
   the old prelude on all three VMs (only the int/tuple lines printed),
   GREEN after, with `star-import-test` and
   `star-import-names-must-be-str-test` still green (JVM, 240 s).
2. New parser packets in `import_programs` (`star-all-main`, `k2`, `ca`,
   `cb`), registered in `e2e_test`'s parser-equality check.
3. `yang.python.antlr.publication` (src): `publication-order`,
   `publish-units!`, `principal-admission`. `yin.repl.link`: `:admit`.
   Fast tests in `dht_import_test` green on the JVM.
4. DONE (JVM). Slow DHT acceptance `dht-import-test-test`, one mesh
   world (publisher A at 1, plain peers 2 and 3, reader at 4; B is a
   second key whose envelopes A's node announces): 29 assertions green,
   6.5 min. Phases:
   - `import m; x = m.val; print(x)` over the DHT source: `["m-body"
     "7"]` on semantic, stack, register (the in-store linked run's and
     CPython's output); `import t` (t imports k) delivers k by name:
     `["k-body" "t-sees 3" "t-done"]`.
   - A republishes k (k2 packet: retraction at seq 5, assertion at 6);
     the reader loads that index; `import t` is refused on every VM with
     `{:reason :yin.link.dht/dependency-binding :name pym.k :pinned k1
     :binding :mismatch :resolved k2}`, thrown as the task's own error
